{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg9y2UrV9irbyMDnLbzJk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed it: fd now gives the \"contains a path-separation character\" error for `--and` patterns too, not just the primary pattern. Formatting, the full test suite and the new test all pass. I haven't committed anything.\n\n**The change** (`src/main.rs`, `ensure_search_pattern_is_not_a_path`): the function now goes through the primary pattern and then each `--and` pattern, and stops at the first one with a path separator. The error names whichever pattern caused it, so `fd foo --and one/two` now reports `'one/two'` instead of searching and finding nothing. The existing rules are unchanged:\n- `--full-path` still turns the check off completely.\n- `/` always triggers the error.\n- On Windows, `\\` only triggers it when the pattern is a real directory on disk, so regex patterns like `\\d+` still work.\n\n**Test** (`tests/tests.rs`): `test_and_pattern_with_forward_slash` checks that the error fires for a `--and` pattern containing `/`, and that it doesn't fire when `--full-path` is used.\n\n**Changelog:** I added a line about this under Bugfixes in `CHANGELOG.md`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":472,"cache_read_input_tokens":37638,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":472,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d4d5b15d-f1c4-4f0f-8505-da71980ba186","uuid":"335230a6-0f98-4728-8432-739262e953c2","timestamp":"2026-10-18T15:49:25.728Z","request_id":"req_011Cg9y2UeaMu7BYoUHWXZEd"}
