 with a path separator. The error names whichever pattern caused it, so `fd foo --and one/two` now reports `'one/two'` instead of searching and finding nothing. The existing rules are unchanged:\n- `--full-path` still turns the check off completely.\n- `/` always triggers the error.\n- On Windows, `\\` only triggers it when the pattern is a real directory on disk, so regex patterns like `\\d+` still work.\n\n**Test** (`tests/tests.rs`): `test_and_pattern_with_forward_slash` checks that the error fires for a `--and` pattern containing `/`, and that it doesn't fire when `--full-path` is used.\n\n**Changelog:** I added a line about this under Bugfixes in `CHANGELOG.md`.","ttft_ms":3278,"type":"result","duration_ms":101442,"uuid":"67cc2801-2995-44fe-b2d0-dc3a2b30ea47","ttft_stream_ms":2653,"time_to_request_ms":169,"first_content_frame_ms":2654,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
